const takeaway = `
<p>Both approaches walk the list once, so the time is the same. What separates them is
what they hold on to: the set remembers every node it passed, which is <code>O(n)</code>
memory on a list that may have ten thousand nodes; the two pointers remember two
references, which is <code>O(1)</code> whatever the length.</p>

<p><strong>Why the two pointers must meet.</strong> Suppose the list does loop. Once both
pointers are inside the loop, look at the distance fast still has to travel forward to
land on slow. Each round slow advances one and fast advances two, so fast closes that
distance by <strong>exactly one</strong> — not two, not a variable amount, one. A quantity
that starts at some whole number and drops by one per round reaches zero, and because it
moves in steps of one it cannot step over zero. Distance zero means the same node. So in a
loop of length <code>L</code> they collide within <code>L</code> rounds; the
<code>gap to close</code> readout in the walkthrough is that number counting down.</p>

<p>The other half of the argument is the easy half: if the list does <em>not</em> loop it
has an end, and fast — moving twice as fast — reaches that end first. Falling off the end
is the proof that there was nothing to be trapped in.</p>

<p>That "gain exactly one per step" argument is not specific to lists. Anything that can
be read as <em>follow a pointer from here</em> has the same shape, which is why
<strong>Find the Duplicate Number</strong> yields to the same trick: treat
<code>i → nums[i]</code> as the next pointer and a duplicate value becomes a node with two
predecessors, which is to say a cycle, detectable without touching the array and without a
byte of extra memory.</p>

<p>One detail worth copying exactly: test <code>fast</code> and <code>fast.next</code>
before taking the two-step move, not after. Check only <code>fast</code> and the second
hop dereferences null on every list of even length.</p>`;
